 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565388 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2e25b77-025f-5093-8bf8-7b6467b74964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565396 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e27cb884-58cc-5969-9740-465e694a1153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565402 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eef364c2-b743-5663-ae5b-bf40b13e0fd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565433 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b04f6fb-738e-5fda-ac4b-d00fe6581051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565433 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +1=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =12>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565451 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2), & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a321d767-b173-540f-8a02-df4dc515c060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565451 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)=0\\,⟹\\,\\pi x=0+k\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=k,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only four lie within the interval $[0,3].$ These are $x=0,1,2,3,$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well, but they are included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1,2,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1))+2)=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (2))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (3))+2)=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565486 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +e)^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},$ the nullity of $(D+eI)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(D+eI)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe770e3a-7d81-54be-9346-77bf6627bbf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565486 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D+eI)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D+eI)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(D+eI)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(D+eI)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D+eI)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D+eI)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(D+eI)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-e.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565490 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19ff9675-2577-5ff5-a7b4-7a7e7b5cf512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565490 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186565433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565491 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a0b64b5-8ad7-595a-ad6a-e5e032ce3417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565491 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186357733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565498 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565498 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32206203-4ed0-5734-9397-48990a5beafb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565498 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565504 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565504 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5373f673-5213-5338-a1d0-efbf6f780dfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565504 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186565421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e9e6af9-ebf7-5415-a0ec-ae18a3d81a55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186354309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565508 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565508 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f56d862-c86a-51b4-936f-ff4084ece379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565508 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186565478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565514 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186565533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565514 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "772a7d55-73b7-505c-b338-1e17c49bf086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565514 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186357720 #edb/r